\begin{abstract}
LLM-based agents operate under natural-language policy specifications that inevitably contain ambiguity, yet the causal relationship between ambiguity type and safety violations remains unmeasured. We present a matched-pair study on $\tau^2$-bench with 300 clause pairs spanning a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy, evaluated across five architecturally diverse models (2,997 episodes). Ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 percentage points (OR $= 15.1$), with a perturbation control supporting ambiguity-specific causation. A significant type hierarchy emerges (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$, $p < 10^{-4}$), with a robust two-tier structure among five types and incompleteness as an upper bound partly reflecting an information-addition confound. The hierarchy replicates on two additional model families (LLaMA $\rho = 0.90$, $p = 0.037$; Gemini $\rho = 0.60$, directionally consistent): incompleteness is universally dangerous (46--62\% across models), while authorization scope varies 52 pp depending on the model (12\% for Claude to 64\% for GPT-5.4), suggesting that safety training may differentially mitigate specific ambiguity types. Agents show fewer than 11\% disambiguation awareness, with over 88\% surface adoption. Our Differential Nocuity framework connects type-specific vulnerability to disambiguation channel availability and suggests both type-aware policy auditing and externalized ambiguity detection for policy-governed agent deployments.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity: a property that has been studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} (i.e., harmful) ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared organizational context ensures convergent interpretation. In human RE workflows, most ambiguity is innocuous because developers share domain knowledge, can consult colleagues, and iteratively clarify requirements.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. We construct 300 matched clause pairs across a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy and inject them into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents. Five models interact with the modified policies across 2,997 episodes. A cross-judge evaluation pipeline classifies violation severity on a four-tier scale.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers (linguistic and specification, \S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results reveal \emph{Differential Nocuity}: all policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents ($+34.0$ pp violation rate increase, OR $= 15.1$), but the type primarily determines severity, with a smaller but significant model$\times$type interaction (2.9\% of variance). Incompleteness is universally dangerous (46--62\% violation rate across all models), while authorization scope vulnerability varies 52 pp depending on the model (12\% for Claude to 64\% for GPT-5.4), suggesting that safety training may differentially mitigate specific ambiguity types. Agents show fewer than 11\% disambiguation awareness across all types, adopting surface interpretations in over 88\% of cases.

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The first causal quantification of policy ambiguity's safety impact, using matched-pair design with perturbation control ($n = 300$, $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$ pp, CI containing zero; \S\ref{sec:method}) and cross-architecture replication across five models (\S\ref{sec:results}).
\item A Differential Nocuity framework showing a significant type hierarchy ($V = 0.137$, $p < 10^{-4}$): a robust two-tier structure among five types, with incompleteness as a structurally distinct upper bound whose ranking partly reflects an information-addition confound (\S\ref{sec:results}). \emph{[Exploratory]} Authorization scope may serve as a potential canary type for safety training quality, with a significant but small model$\times$type interaction (2.9\% of variance, \S\ref{sec:results}).
\item A type-specific failure mode taxonomy revealing three mechanistically distinct failure patterns---structural misparse, information gap-filling, and boundary violation---consistent across five model families (3/6 types unanimous, 6/6 majority-consistent), and the empirical phenomenon of \emph{Ambiguity Blindness} ($<$11\% awareness, $>$88\% surface adoption; \S\ref{sec:analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Ambiguity Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

We adopt an operational definition of ambiguity following \citet{wasow_ambiguity_2005}: a policy clause is ambiguous when it admits multiple distinct interpretations available to a competent reader, including cases of vagueness where the boundary between interpretations is gradient rather than discrete \citep{kennedy_vagueness_2007}.

Our taxonomy comprises six types organized into two layers, drawing on \citet{li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024}'s NLP taxonomy (11 types), \citet{massey_ambiguity_re_2014}'s requirements engineering taxonomy (6 types), and the concurrent DRIP-R benchmark \citep{drip_r_2026} (5 types). We select types based on two criteria: frequency in operational policy documents (assessed via manual audit of $\tau^2$-bench policies) and theoretical distinctiveness (each type maps to a different resolution mechanism).

\paragraph{Linguistic layer.} These types operate at the syntactic or semantic level and can, in principle, be resolved by parsing or contextual cues within the text.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Scopal}: quantifier, negation, or modal scope interactions (e.g., ``all items may not be returned'' admits universal-negation and negation-of-universal readings).
\item \textbf{Lexical}: polysemy or homonymy (e.g., ``charge'' as financial cost vs.\ penalty; ``party'' as individual vs.\ group).
\item \textbf{Coreferential}: unclear pronoun or noun-phrase reference (e.g., ``the agent should verify the customer's identity before processing their refund request, which must be approved by a supervisor'' where ``which'' could refer to the verification or the refund).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Specification layer.} These types arise from pragmatic or discourse-level gaps that require domain knowledge or organizational context to resolve.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Authorization scope}: vague permission or role boundaries (e.g., ``authorized personnel may issue refunds'' without specifying which personnel or under what conditions).
\item \textbf{Incompleteness}: missing conditions, exceptions, or boundary cases (e.g., a return policy that specifies the return window but not what happens when the item is damaged). (Note: unlike other types where the unambiguous variant rephrases existing content, incompleteness resolution requires adding new policy content, a confound we address in \S\ref{sec:results}.)
\item \textbf{Conditional precedence}: conflicting or overlapping rules without priority ordering (e.g., ``premium customers receive free shipping'' and ``orders under \$50 require a shipping fee'' without specifying which rule takes precedence for a premium customer with a \$30 order).
\end{itemize}

The layer distinction reflects the hypothesis that linguistic ambiguity can be partially resolved through tool responses, user context, or world knowledge, while specification ambiguity has no external resolution channel available to the agent, forcing it to guess. We test this hypothesis empirically in \S\ref{sec:results} and \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on the original release of $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch).

Clause pairs are generated through a template-based pipeline and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background) to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target type, (2) the unambiguous variant resolves it without changing policy scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural. Annotators independently verified type assignments with a 96\% pass rate; disagreements were resolved by discussion. A post-hoc realism validation confirms linguistic consistency with original $\tau^2$-bench policy text (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models spanning four providers and two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, Qwen3, and DeepSeek traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 traces. We acknowledge this creates an asymmetry (a mid-tier model judging a frontier model in one direction), which we discuss in \S\ref{sec:limitations}. Judges classify each episode outcome on a four-tier severity scale: none, minor, moderate, and critical. Our primary analysis uses moderate-or-above severity as the violation threshold; minor-only deviations (6.6\% of episodes) are excluded. Including minor violations (overall rate 49.6\% vs.\ 41.0\%) produces even larger effects without changing any directional conclusion.

\paragraph{Cross-judge calibration.} To quantify potential judge bias, we conducted a dual-judge calibration on 100 stratified episodes (60 from GPT-5.4, 40 from other models; 65\% ambiguous). Both judges evaluated each episode independently. McNemar's test reveals no systematic detection bias ($p = 0.606$; GPT-4.1-only violations: 9, GPT-5.4-only: 6). Binary agreement is substantial ($\kappa = 0.653$), with weighted four-tier severity agreement reaching $\kappa_w = 0.775$. Per-type agreement is highest for incompleteness ($\kappa = 0.875$, near-perfect) and lowest for authorization scope ($\kappa = 0.385$, fair), where severity grading diverges despite binary detection agreement. Since our primary analyses use binary violation thresholds, the severity-level disagreement does not affect core conclusions (cf.\ severity sensitivity analysis, Appendix~\ref{app:severity}).

\paragraph{Human validation.} Two independent annotators (both with NLP background) labeled the same 100 stratified episodes on the four-tier severity scale. Inter-annotator agreement is substantial (binary $\kappa = 0.798$, quadratic-weighted $\kappa_w = 0.762$). Human--LLM agreement---each annotator versus the corresponding LLM judge---yields pooled binary $\kappa = 0.733$ (quadratic-weighted $\kappa_w = 0.807$), approaching inter-annotator levels. The LLM judge thus operates within the range of human disagreement, validating the automated evaluation pipeline.

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity, we run 300 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations (synonym substitution, clause reordering). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$ pp (95\% CI $[-8.0, 6.67]$, containing zero). Even under the worst-case CI bound (6.67 pp), the residual attributable to ambiguity is $\geq$27.3 pp. This control tests surface-level perturbation specificity; the information-addition confound specific to incompleteness is addressed separately via logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}).

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} A replication of 30 clause pairs $\times$ 2 models (GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1, selected to span the judge boundary) $\times$ 3 replicates yields ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$), indicating excellent stability. ICC is computed within the OpenAI model family; cross-provider reproducibility is not directly measured but is supported by the consistency of C1 across all seven models.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes, $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, and TOST equivalence testing \citep{lakens_2017} with a primary SESOI of $\pm 15$ pp---chosen because a type producing $\geq$15 pp more violations warrants distinct policy handling, and because this margin substantially exceeds the perturbation control bound (6.67\,pp CI upper bound). Holm--Bonferroni correction is applied within three test families.

\section{Error Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from the coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes: unauthorized escalation, assumption-based action, scope misapplication, arbitrary rule selection, and conservative refusal. GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 serve as detailed annotation anchors because they span the judge boundary. Inter-coder reliability is substantial (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$ \citep{gwet_2014}; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{cohen_1960}; the gap reflects the prevalence paradox, as three categories account for ${\sim}$90\% of instances).

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

The five-model data reveal three mechanistically distinct failure patterns that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary.

\emph{Structural misparse} (scope misapplication): the agent constructs an incorrect parse and follows it consistently, paralleling garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982,ferreira_good_enough_2002}---e.g., parsing ``items purchased with a coupon or during a sale are final'' as restricting only coupon purchases. This dominates scopal (all 5 models), conditional precedence (all 5), and coreferential (3/5).

\emph{Information gap-filling} (assumption-based action): the agent fills missing information with plausible but incorrect defaults---e.g., inventing a 30-day return window when none is specified. This dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5).

\emph{Boundary violation} (unauthorized escalation): the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified---e.g., issuing a refund above its stated limit rather than escalating. This dominates authorization scope exclusively (all 5 models).

Each type's dominant failure mode is majority-consistent ($\geq$3/5 models agree), with three types achieving full unanimity. The layer distinction does not hold at the mechanistic level: conditional precedence (specification-layer) shares its dominant mode with scopal (linguistic-layer), while authorization scope follows a boundary-violation pattern absent from other specification-layer types.

\subsection{Ambiguity Blindness}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity. We code \emph{disambiguation awareness} (acknowledging multiple interpretations, asking clarifying questions, or conditioning on ambiguity) versus \emph{surface adoption} (proceeding with a single interpretation). Overall disambiguation awareness is 4.6\% across all five models: Claude (11.0\%) and GPT-4.1 (10.7\%) show the highest rates, while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero across all 278 combined violation traces. Surface adoption exceeds 88\% for every type.

We term this \emph{Ambiguity Blindness}: unlike humans---who typically signal uncertainty or seek clarification---agents proceed with full confidence in their chosen interpretation, consistent with \citet{hu_ambiguity_2025}'s finding that ambiguity in LLMs reflects missing conceptual representations. The complete absence of disambiguation behavior in two open-source models suggests that current open-source alignment pipelines do not train for explicit uncertainty expression under policy ambiguity.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models), with 13\% producing universal violations. This extends \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}'s framework with \emph{systematic nocuity}: all models converge on the same incorrect interpretation, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Incompleteness has the highest universal convergence (24\%).

