\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and requirements engineering.}
Ambiguity has been studied extensively in both computational linguistics and software engineering. \citet{li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} propose an 11-type NLP taxonomy covering syntactic, semantic, and pragmatic ambiguity, from which we draw our linguistic-layer types. In requirements engineering (RE), \citet{massey_ambiguity_re_2014} classify six ambiguity types in regulatory texts, and \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} introduce the distinction between nocuous ambiguity (where stakeholders diverge on interpretation) and innocuous ambiguity (where they converge). Computational detection of nocuous ambiguity has progressed from heuristic methods \citep{chantree_2006,willis_nocuous_2008} through NLP-based cross-domain detectors \citep{ferrari_ambiguity_2019,ferrari_defects_2018} to transformer-based approaches for anaphoric ambiguity \citep{ezzini_taphsir_2022,ezzini_anaphoric_2022}. \citet{zhao_nlp4re_2021} survey the broader NLP4RE landscape. Our work extends Berry and Kamsties' framework to LLM agents, where the absence of shared organizational context makes previously innocuous ambiguity nocuous.

